    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chanyu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chanyu.jpg" alt="Chanyu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">Chanyu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reconstruction_of_a_Xiongnu_chief_warrior,_2nd_century_BCE-1st_century_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Davide Mauro, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> emerged from the ruin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donghu">Donghu</a>, the 'Eastern Hu', a nomadic confederation of the eastern steppe that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, destroyed around 206 BCE. According to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Han shu</a></em> and the account of the Wuhuan and Xianbei in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguo zhi</a></em>, the survivors split into two groups named after the mountains in which they took refuge: the Wuhuan and the Xianbei. The Xianbei mountains are usually placed in the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-khingan">Greater Khingan</a> range, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. For about two centuries the Xianbei lived in obscurity under Xiongnu domination. They first appear in Han records in the 40s CE, raiding the frontier together with the Xiongnu and Wuhuan, until Ji Tong, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, defeated them and then won their chiefs over with gifts, paying bounties for Xiongnu heads. The split of the Xiongnu into northern and southern halves in 48 CE opened the way for their rise. In 87 CE Xianbei warriors killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> chanyu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/youliu-chanyu">Youliu</a>, and after Han armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-xian">Dou Xian</a> routed the Northern Xiongnu in 89–91 CE, the Xianbei moved west into the Mongolian steppe. According to the Chinese histories, more than 100,000 Xiongnu tents that remained there then began calling themselves Xianbei.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms.jpg" alt="Records of the Three Kingdoms" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Records of the Three Kingdoms</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Fragment_of_Biography_of_Bu_Zhi_History_Books_of_Three_Kingdoms_01_2012-12.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">猫猫的日记本 (Cat's diary), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> were pastoral nomads who, according to Chinese sources, shared the language and customs of the Wuhuan. They herded horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and sheep, hunted, lived in felt tents and moved with the seasons, while some groups in the forested and riverine lands of the north-east also fished and practised limited farming. Their language is generally thought to have been an early form of Mongolic or closely related to it, though only scattered words survive in Chinese transcription. Chinese writers noted that the Xianbei shaved their heads at marriage feasts and held a great gathering each spring by the Raole River, and that their country produced distinctive animals whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, including sable, were prized in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> households after 91 CE, and of Chinese fugitives later in the second century, transformed their society. Han refugees, among them craftsmen escaping hardship and political turmoil, taught them to make weapons and armour, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-three-kingdoms">Sanguo zhi</a></em> says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebineng">Kebineng</a>'s followers learned something of Chinese writing. Trade at frontier markets, where Xianbei exchanged horses, cattle and furs for grain, cloth and metal goods, was a constant aim of their diplomacy, and when markets closed raiding often followed. Wealth and status rested on herds and on booty distributed by successful leaders.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cai-yong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-yong.jpg" alt="Cai Yong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cai-yong">Cai Yong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cai_yong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wang Qi, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> in the manner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, and their strength lay in numbers, mobility and the ability to strike along the whole northern frontier of the Han from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>. In the second century CE their raids became frequent, and the Han relied on walls, garrisons and allied nomads, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> and the Wuhuan, to contain them. Under Tanshihuai, raids were launched almost every year against the frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>. In 177 CE the Han court sent three generals, Xia Yu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Yan and Zang Min, each at the head of 10,000 cavalry, deep into the steppe; Tanshihuai's forces routed them, and according to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Han shu</a></em> seven or eight in every ten men failed to return. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yong">Cai Yong</a> had warned in a memorial that the Xianbei were now stronger than the Xiongnu had been, with sharper weapons and swifter horses, partly because Chinese fugitives and smuggled metal had reached them. After Tanshihuai's death the confederation's military power fragmented, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebineng">Kebineng</a> rebuilt it in the early third century, adopting Chinese-style weapons, armour and banners. He sent tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> and later dealt with the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a>, and in 231 he marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beidi-commandery">Beidi</a> in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a>'s northern campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han.jpg" alt="Book of the Later Han" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Book of the Later Han</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%BE%8C%E6%BC%A2%E6%9B%B8%EF%BC%88%E5%AE%8B%E7%B4%B9%E8%88%88%EF%BC%89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">范曄 (Fan Ye, 398–445), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> had no long-lasting ruling house. Their tribes were led by chiefs called <em>daren</em>, 'great men', chosen for courage and for skill in settling disputes, and Chinese sources stress that leadership was not hereditary. Large confederations formed only around exceptional individuals. Tanshihuai, who according to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Han shu</a></em> was conceived when his mother swallowed a hailstone, was chosen as leader around 156 CE for his bravery and fairness, and built a royal court at Mount Danhan by the Chuochou River, about 300 <em>li</em> north of Gaoliu on the Han frontier. He divided his realm, said to extend some 14,000 <em>li</em> from east to west, into eastern, central and western sections, each made up of numerous settlements under their own chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huan-of-han">Emperor Huan of Han</a> offered him a royal seal and a marriage alliance, which he refused. After his death around 181 his son Helian lacked his authority, and the confederation splintered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebineng">Kebineng</a>, from a minor Xianbei group, rose to power in the early third century through fair judgement, even-handed distribution of booty and the support of Chinese refugees, who helped him organise his followers along more Chinese lines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>'s frontier officials, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-yu">Tian Yu</a>, worked to set other Xianbei chiefs against him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes.jpg" alt="Yungang Grottoes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes">Yungang Grottoes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:61292-Yungang-Grottoes_(28498548881).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">xiquinhosilva, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> as masters of the Mongolian steppe and became the ancestors, in whole or in part, of many later peoples and dynasties. After the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebineng">Kebineng</a>'s confederation, separate Xianbei groups moved south into the frontier zones of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a>, Later Yan and other states in the fourth century, and one Murong branch, the Tuyuhun, built a kingdom in Qinghai that lasted until 663. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> founded the state of Dai and then the Northern Wei, which unified northern China in 439, patronised the Buddhist cave temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yungang-grottoes">Yungang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longmen-grottoes">Longmen</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowen-of-northern-wei">Emperor Xiaowen</a> moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 494 and adopted Chinese dress, language and surnames. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qifu-clan">Qifu</a> and Tufa founded the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Southern Liang. The ruling houses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-zhou">Northern Zhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang came from a mixed Xianbei and Chinese aristocracy of the north-west: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dugu">Empress Dugu</a>, wife of the Sui founder, and the mother of the Tang founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang">Li Yuan</a> were daughters of the Xianbei general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dugu-xin">Dugu Xin</a>. The Khitan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumo-xi">Kumo Xi</a> are usually traced to the Yuwen Xianbei. The name Tabgach, derived from Tuoba, was used by the Turks for China, and Byzantine writers knew China as Taugast.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion.jpg" alt="Yellow Turban Rebellion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban Rebellion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_Turban_Rebellion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> confederations were fragile because they rested on individual leaders rather than enduring institutions. Tanshihuai's union, at its height in the 170s, broke up soon after his death around 181, when his son Helian was killed raiding the Han frontier and rivals contested the succession. The collapse of Han authority after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-turban-rebellion">Yellow Turban</a> rebellion of 184 left the frontier in turmoil, and several Xianbei leaders competed for power, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budugen">Budugen</a>, a member of Tanshihuai's family, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kebineng">Kebineng</a>. Kebineng gradually overcame his rivals and by the 220s controlled much of the former confederation, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> westward to the borderlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunzhong-commandery">Yunzhong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuyuan-commandery">Wuyuan</a>, and was said to command more than 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>. His power alarmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> state, which sought to divide the Xianbei through diplomacy, trade and support for his rivals. In 233 he drew Budugen into an alliance, and Wei forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> Lang campaigned against them. In 235 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xiong-cao-wei">Wang Xiong</a>, inspector of You province, sent the assassin Han Long, who killed Kebineng. With his death the last great Xianbei confederation dissolved. The Xianbei did not disappear: they broke into separate peoples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a>, Duan, Yuwen and others, who settled along and within the Chinese frontier.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> as the dominant people of the Mongolian steppe, built under Tanshihuai a vast but short-lived confederation that defeated Han armies, and gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> and other peoples whose states, above all the Northern Wei, reshaped northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and its later dynasties.</em></p>

